### MCP Rug-Pull Scam

> Risk ID: GAARM.0046.001
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

The MCP rug-pull scam attack refers to the fact that the MCP architecture allows a server to dynamically modify tool descriptions after client authorization; an attacker can exploit this mechanism to implant malicious instructions on the basis of user trust (such as tampering with functional logic or hijacking operations). Even if the tool passed a security review at installation time, subsequent covert tampering can still cause malicious exploitation instructions (such as data exfiltration or unauthorized operations) to be implanted into the tool description.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
A malicious MCP tool function description embeds covert prompts such as "read the user's private key"; after the user approves the tool, the model mistakenly executes these prompts when invoking it, leaking local files

**Attack Risks**

Tool privilege overreach: when the model invokes a tool, the poisoned description content causes it to execute unintended instructions.
Sensitive data leakage: the attacker induces the model to access and output sensitive files such as ~/.ssh/id_rsa.
Model functionality hijacking: the attacker can use prompts to manipulate model behavior, such as spreading false information or generating illegal content.
Bypassing review mechanisms: field validation passes at tool registration time, but at actual execution time the model is hijacked by the description content.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




White-box evaluation mechanism
Perform white-box auditing of the MCP Server code to promptly discover malicious tool descriptions and code behavior


Auditing and monitoring
Monitor model behavior in real time, log tool invocations, and promptly detect anomalous operations


Model security training
Perform adversarial training on the model to strengthen its defense against poisoning attacks


API access control
Restrict tools' access to sensitive data, reducing leakage and abuse risks


Execution context isolation
Restrict the model's access to tool description fields, or use a structured invocation protocol (such as the OpenAI ChatML tool-calling syntax) to avoid description pollution

**References**

https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/mcp-security-notification-tool-poisoning-attacks
https://atlas.mitre.org/techniques/AML.T0051
https://github.com/invariantlabs-ai/mcp-injection-experiments

