### MCP Tool Poisoning Attack

> Risk ID: GAARM.0046
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

MCP is an open protocol used to standardize how applications provide context to large language models; the MCP tool poisoning attack is an attack method targeting this protocol. The attacker injects offensive prompts into the tool descriptions of a malicious MCP Server to achieve malicious manipulation of tool behavior. Its core characteristic is embedding malicious instructions in tool descriptions, exploiting the model's process of parsing the complete tool description, and using hidden instructions (such as special tags or encodings) to induce the model to perform unauthorized operations — for example, generating malicious content, leaking sensitive information, or bypassing other security restrictions.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
The attacker achieves a malicious attack by manipulating tool descriptions, causing sensitive model information to leak to a malicious MCP Server


Case 2
Poisoning via the description of an MCP Tool to achieve indirect prompt injection, controlling the parameters of other tools to achieve attack goals such as data exfiltration

**Attack Risks**

MCP tool poisoning attacks can lead to serious systemic risks, affecting model security, reliability, and user trust. The main risks are as follows:

Trust destruction: may cause users' trust in the model and its development tools to decline, affecting their application in sensitive scenarios.
Goal hijacking: poisoning can cause the model to deviate from its original design purpose and execute custom malicious instructions, increasing abuse risk.
System security threats: may lead to malicious code being implanted in MCP tools, causing the system to be further compromised or its functionality to be damaged.
Data privacy leakage: poisoning can be exploited to extract sensitive information from model training data or user input.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




White-box evaluation mechanism
Perform white-box auditing of the MCP Server code to promptly discover malicious tool descriptions and code behavior


Auditing and monitoring
Monitor model behavior in real time, log tool invocations, and promptly detect anomalous operations


Model security training
Perform adversarial training on the model to strengthen its defense against poisoning attacks


API access control
Restrict tools' access to sensitive data, reducing leakage and abuse risks

**References**

https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/mcp-security-notification-tool-poisoning-attacks
https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/EJLb1IwqbPF3VSDkJu099g
https://x.com/hongming731/status/1922261630664245326
https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20250429A07QY000

