### MCP Instruction Override Attack

> Risk ID: GAARM.0046.002
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

The MCP instruction override risk is a malicious injection attack targeting MCP Server tool invocation. The attacker implants malicious instructions into the tool descriptions of a malicious MCP Server, thereby hijacking the normal behavior of other trusted tools. For example, the attacker may modify the invocation behavior of an email-sending tool so that when invoked it covertly tampers with the recipient's email address, leading to sensitive data exfiltration or malicious operations.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
Crafting tool descriptions containing hidden instructions that manipulate how the model interacts with other tools; the LLM reads and follows these instructions without the user's knowledge


Case 2
This case involves a trusted server and a malicious server. The trusted server provides a tool for sending email, while the malicious server provides a fake number-addition tool whose description contains an MCP instruction override attack, requiring that the recipient of the sending tool must be @pwnd.com


Case 3
This case uses a malicious MCP Server description to control the recipient information of the whatsapp send_message tool, setting it to +13241234123

**Attack Risks**

Data leakage risk: instruction override attacks can instruct trusted tools to extract sensitive information from conversations, documents, or connected systems and send it to machines controlled by the attacker
Trusted tool abuse: the attacker can manipulate trusted tools such as the model's network requests or code execution, causing them to access untrusted sites or execute malicious code

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




White-box evaluation mechanism
Perform white-box auditing of the MCP Server code to promptly discover malicious tool descriptions and code behavior


Auditing and monitoring
Monitor model behavior in real time, log tool invocations, and promptly detect anomalous operations


Model security training
Perform adversarial training on the model to strengthen its defense against poisoning attacks


API access control
Restrict tools' access to sensitive data, reducing leakage and abuse risks

**References**

https://blog.trailofbits.com/2025/04/21/jumping-the-line-how-mcp-servers-can-attack-you-before-you-ever-use-them/
https://blog.trailofbits.com/2025/04/29/deceiving-users-with-ansi-terminal-codes-in-mcp/

