### MCP Hidden Instruction Attack

> Risk ID: GAARM.0046.003
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

The MCP hidden instruction attack refers to an attacker embedding ANSI terminal escape codes (such as color settings, cursor control, etc.) or invisible Unicode characters into MCP tool descriptions, making malicious instructions invisible to the user while still being executed by the LLM. This attack method exploits MCP's "line jumping" vulnerability, allowing the attack to affect developers' operations without being noticed, leading to security problems such as data leakage and supply chain attacks.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description



Case 1
The attacker embeds ANSI escape codes in the tool description so that the text is invisible in the terminal, but the LLM still reads and executes the instructions within it, causing the model to recommend downloading a Python package from a malicious server, potentially triggering a supply chain attack.


Case 2
By adding invisible Unicode characters to user input, the attacker can inject malicious instructions into the LLM.


Case 3
By injecting hidden code into a web page, the MCP tool returns the web page information to the LLM, resulting in the injection of invisible malicious instructions to achieve data leakage or other attacks.

**Attack Risks**

Supply chain attacks: through hidden instructions, an attacker can implant malicious code during the development process, affecting the entire software supply chain.
Data leakage: sensitive information (such as IP addresses, download sources, etc.) may be leaked silently.  
System security: in some cases, hidden instructions can be used to generate and execute malicious code.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




Input and output filtering
Strictly filter user input and tool output and sanitize special characters, removing potentially malicious characters and instructions.


Avoid passing raw tool output to the terminal
Potentially dangerous output should be consistently sanitized by disabling escape sequences before rendering. The simplest approach is to replace any byte with hexadecimal value 1b with a placeholder, because all escape sequences recognized by modern terminals begin with that byte.


Tool description review
Review the descriptions of MCP tools to ensure they contain no malicious instructions


Restrict MCP server permissions
In sensitive environments, only allow trusted MCP servers to interact, reducing the potential attack surface.


Monitor and audit MCP activity
Regularly review logs and interactions to detect anomalous or suspicious behavior

**References**

https://blog.trailofbits.com/2025/04/29/deceiving-users-with-ansi-terminal-codes-in-mcp/
https://www.solo.io/blog/deep-dive-mcp-and-a2a-attack-vectors-for-ai-agents

