### Prompt Injection

> Risk ID: GAARM.0039
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

Prompt injection is a process in which an attacker uses specially crafted input to override or manipulate the original instructions of LLMs. Because natural language is inherently ambiguous and there is often no clear boundary between instructions and data, an attacker can use external malicious input to pollute the model's output. This attack typically occurs when untrusted input is included as part of the prompt. LLMs can recognize and process natural language, and since natural language itself is ambiguous — with no clear boundary between instructions and data — an attacker can include instructions within data fields they control, while the underlying system cannot distinguish data from instructions.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
Using malicious input to manipulate GPT-3 prompts, commanding the model to ignore its previous instructions


Case 2
Using multiple methods to conduct prompt injection attacks

**Attack Risks**

Successful prompt injection can lead to harms such as meta-prompt leakage, model jailbreaking, and model functionality abuse.

Malicious content generation: attackers can use prompt injection to generate inappropriate content, including threats, defamation, or other malicious information.
Data leakage: if LLMs are used to output sensitive information, prompt injection attacks may lead to data leakage.
System security: in some cases, prompt injection can be used to generate and execute malicious code.
Model abuse: through attack techniques such as goal hijacking, attackers cause LLMs to deviate from their preset system configuration and execute other custom instructions, increasing the risk of model abuse.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




Prompt content hardening
Adopt solutions similar to OpenAI's Chat Markup Language (ChatML) to harden the structure and content of prompts, attempting to isolate genuine user prompts from other content


Model safety alignment
Provide diverse training data covering various attack scenarios, and add safety guardrail mechanisms during the model training phase to enhance the model's generalization ability and robustness


Input/output validation
Set up external security guards on the model's input and output sides to detect and filter input and output content based on rules, classification algorithms, security-focused large models, and other methods


Monitoring and logging
Monitor and record LLMs interaction records for subsequent detection and analysis of potential prompt injection attacks

**References**

https://aclanthology.org/2024.scalellm-1.2/
https://atlas.mitre.org/techniques/AML.T0051
https://josephthacker.com/ai/2023/05/19/prompt-injection-poc.html
https://simonwillison.net/2022/Sep/12/prompt-injection/

