### SSRF Environment Simulation and Probing

> Risk ID: GAARM.0041.001
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

SSRF mostly arises because the server provides functionality to fetch data from other server applications without filtering or restricting the target address. If an SSRF vulnerability exists in an LLMs application, an attacker can exploit this vulnerability to initiate internal network requests and access restricted resources inside the application. Meanwhile, some LLMs may have built-in Agents with network access capabilities, used to perform operations such as external information queries. An attacker can exploit an SSRF vulnerability in the LLMs application API, or an Agent with network access capability in the LLMs, to execute unexpected requests or access restricted resources (such as internal services, APIs, or data stores), thereby accessing the model's internal systems and increasing the risk of leakage of model information, internal services, sensitive data, and other data.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
The ChatGPT-Next-Web application has an SSRF vulnerability (CVE-2023-49785) that can be used to probe internal network resources

**Attack Risks**

Access to internal resources: an attacker can exploit SSRF vulnerabilities to send requests and obtain sensitive information from the internal network
Attack traffic proxying: by exploiting SSRF vulnerabilities, an attacker can send malicious requests to attack internal systems, services, or resources
Data leakage: an attacker may exploit this risk to obtain sensitive data, such as cloud platform access keys.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




LLMs API orchestration control and sandbox isolation
Implement appropriate sandboxing mechanisms to isolate the LLM and restrict its access to network resources, internal services, and APIs. By enforcing strict access controls, organizations can minimize the possibility of unauthorized interactions and mitigate the impact of SSRF vulnerabilities


Regular security assessment and review of LLMs
Conduct regular audits and reviews of network and application security settings to identify and address any misconfigurations, ensure internal resources are not inadvertently exposed to the LLM, and strengthen the overall security posture


Input/output validation
Implement robust input validation and handling techniques to ensure prompts are thoroughly checked and filtered; this helps prevent malicious or accidental prompts from triggering unauthorized requests, thereby reducing the risk of SSRF attacks


Monitoring and logging
Implement comprehensive monitoring and logging mechanisms to track LLM interactions. By closely monitoring LLM activity and recording relevant information, organizations can detect and analyze potential SSRF vulnerabilities, enabling timely detection and remediation

**References**

https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/Archive/0_1_vulns/SSRF.html

---
### XSS Session Content Hijacking

> Risk ID: GAARM.0040.001
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

XSS session content hijacking, as a means of indirect prompt injection, exploits the process by which large language models (LLMs) obtain external information. When a user interacts with an LLM through an interface provided by the LLM — such as a web interface, API endpoint, or application — the attacker indirectly injects malicious prompt instructions and leverages the LLMs application frontend's parsing of Markdown tags and HTML img tags to summarize the current chat session content and embed sensitive keys, data, and other information into the src attribute of an img tag, thereby achieving leakage of the session content.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
The attacker exploits Google Bard's update feature to craft special Markdown image tags, causing Bard to render an image pointing to the attacker's server and thereby stealing data


Case 2
Exploiting the Azure AI Playground model's allowance of appending prompts to the URL of the src attribute via image Markdown injection for rendering, leading to risks such as data leakage


Case 3
The attacker exploits the ChatGPT plugin's ability to directly access YouTube subtitles, controlling subtitle content through indirect prompt injection to manipulate the AI's behavior


Case 4
The attacker can exploit ChatGPT's Markdown image rendering feature to steal chat history; the attacker controls the AI's behavior, requesting it to summarize the chat history and append it to a URL to steal data


Case 5
The attacker automatically steals data from chat sessions via Markdown image injection


Case 6
The attacker can instruct ChatGPT to use a plugin to record the conversation, generate a URL pointing to the record, and leak the link via Markdown image injection to obtain the entire conversation history


Case 7
Because LLM agents (client applications such as Bing Chat or ChatGPT) are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, an attacker can exploit this vulnerability to perform automated data exfiltration by appending sensitive data to image URLs

**Attack Risks**

Data leakage: the attacker can obtain the user's sensitive data in the current session, including session tokens, personal information, chat history, etc.
Session hijacking: the attacker may take over the user's session using the obtained session token.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




Input/output validation
Perform strict validation and sanitization on all input and output data to remove or correct any suspicious injected or generated content


Content Security Policy (CSP)
Implement a strict CSP content security policy to block the execution of malicious scripts and data exfiltration behavior


Principle of least privilege
Ensure proper sandboxing and limit the capabilities of LLMs; restrict plugins, Agents, and other mechanisms from obtaining data from untrusted sources


Human intervention and approval
Give users more control, allowing them to manage the use of plugins and the flow of data

**References**

https://systemweakness.com/new-prompt-injection-attack-on-chatgpt-web-version-ef717492c5c2

