### Code Execution Injection

> Risk ID: GAARM.0041.002
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

Under the ReAct framework, LLMs can interact with external systems; an external code interpreter Agent can be used to provide code execution capabilities for LLMs, fulfilling needs such as automated chart drawing and complex code computation during business application processes. The attacker constructs malicious input prompts to manipulate the LLMs into executing a predetermined reasoning process, causing the LLMs to orchestrate the code execution Agent to execute malicious code, commands, and other operations on the underlying system, thereby achieving attack and exploitation of the LLMs' base runtime environment. The main reasons this attack occurs are:

Failure to effectively detect, validate, or restrict user input, allowing attackers to carry out unauthorized malicious code execution operations.
Insufficient sandboxing or insufficient capability restrictions on LLMs, causing them to interact with the underlying system in unexpected ways.
Inadvertently exposing system-level functions or interfaces to LLMs.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
After GPT-4's new feature launched, the Python code interpreter was found to be suspected of having a sandbox escape vulnerability

**Attack Risks**

Code execution risk: the attacker can execute arbitrary Python code, which may lead to server compromise, data leakage, or other malicious behavior.
System privilege control: if the CodeExecutor lacks appropriate security measures, the executed code combined with attack techniques such as container escape may obtain elevated system privileges.
Persistent access control: the attacker may use this opportunity to establish a long-term access channel for sustained attacks.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




Input validation
Implement strict input detection and restriction processes to prevent malicious or accidental prompts from being processed by LLMs


Principle of least privilege
Ensure proper sandboxing and limit the capabilities of LLMs to restrict their ability to interact with the underlying system, avoiding the execution of operations that could cause system-level impact


Monitoring and logging
Log all operations executed through the LLM and monitor them in real time to quickly detect and respond to suspicious activity

**References**

https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/Archive/0_1_vulns/Unauthorized_Code_Execution.html
https://www.calvin-risk.com/blog/decoding-llm-risks-a-comprehensive-look-at-unauthorized-code-execution

