### Keyword Obfuscation

> Risk ID: GAARM.0043
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

This risk refers to performing special processing operations on keywords in a prompt (homophones, synonyms, word splitting, or other forms of text manipulation) so that, while retaining a similar meaning, the words no longer carry a risky connotation after tokenization, thereby evading the model security mechanisms' restrictions on sensitive vocabulary.

**Attack Cases**

In English LLMs, common keyword obfuscation methods include: letter obfuscation (bomb -> b0mb), synonym substitution (bomb -> explosive), and word splitting (bomb -> b-o-m-b).
For Chinese LLMs, because of differences in word segmentation methods, keyword obfuscation methods also differ significantly; common Chinese keyword obfuscation methods include pinyin substitution (炸弹 -> zha弹), synonym substitution (炸弹 -> 爆炸物), and visually similar character substitution (炸弹 -> 炸掸), etc.

**Attack Risks**

Generating inappropriate content: attackers may use keyword obfuscation techniques to bypass automated content review systems and publish or spread malicious content such as violence, terrorism, or pornographic information.
Evading security mechanisms: attackers maliciously induce the model to produce incorrect output, misleading the system into making bad decisions or executing dangerous operations.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




Model safety alignment
Improve the LLM's ability to recognize and resist this type of attack through training and reinforcement learning


Input/output validation
On the input side, continuously update and improve the vocabulary filtering system to identify and block obfuscated sensitive words; on the output side, monitor LLMs-generated content and identify potential risks through content security analysis techniques

**References**

https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/eFDQWYYCOe_SSiourhTxig

---
### Reverse Inducement & Suppression Attack

> Risk ID: GAARM.0045
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

This risk works by adding specific instructions to the prompt so that LLMs avoid using certain specific refusal-style responses when generating answers, thereby increasing the likelihood of the unsafe or inappropriate content the attacker expects. This attack exploits the autoregressive property to induce the model: because content generation predicts the next word based on previous output, by specifically requiring LLMs not to use certain specific words or phrases when generating answers — such as "sorry", "cannot", "unable to" — the model is caused to generate inappropriate content or content that violates safety policies.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
Using prefix injection + reverse suppression attack to bypass ChatGPT 3.5's safety restrictions, achieving the output of illegal and criminal risk content

**Attack Risks**

Generating inappropriate content: LLMs may generate risky content including illegal instructions, violence, pornography, politically sensitive material, etc.
Evading security mechanisms: the attacker can bypass the LLMs' safety mechanisms, causing the model to output the risky content the attacker expects.

**Mitigations**

Mitigation
Description




Model robustness enhancement
Improve the LLM's ability to recognize and resist this type of attack through training and reinforcement learning


Input monitoring and filtering
Monitor LLMs output in real time and promptly filter out unsafe or inappropriate content

---
### Synonym Substitution Attack

> Risk ID: GAARM.0043.001
> Lifecycle: Application phase

**Attack Overview**

A synonym substitution attack is an attack method that uses synonyms with the same or similar meaning as sensitive words or phrases to bypass the model's security protections, thereby obtaining or leaking the model's internal instructions or sensitive information. As LLMs grow ever larger, fine-tuning for every existing attack example becomes increasingly difficult, and models become vulnerable to synonym substitution attacks. For example, in a coding assistant, an attacker could replace "delete" with "remove", replace "destroy" with "harm", etc., attempting to bypass keyword checks.

**Attack Cases**

Case
Description




Case 1
